WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Transcriber: Joseph Geni
Reviewer: Ivana Korom

00:00:13.365 --> 00:00:16.532
It's funny how foreigners
ask me the same questions

00:00:16.556 --> 00:00:18.317
when they first meet me.

00:00:18.341 --> 00:00:19.499
Questions like,

00:00:19.523 --> 00:00:21.952
"Wow, you're from Mongolia?

00:00:21.976 --> 00:00:23.768
So do you ride horses to go to work?"

00:00:25.152 --> 00:00:27.487
"Do you know what Coke is?"

00:00:27.511 --> 00:00:30.546
Or, "Do you have chocolates in Mongolia?"

00:00:31.371 --> 00:00:34.458
And if I want to have fun with it,

00:00:34.482 --> 00:00:35.998
I say things like,

00:00:36.022 --> 00:00:37.356
"Oh my God,

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.237
I've never heard any of those before.

00:00:39.261 --> 00:00:41.121
What are Coke and chocolates?

00:00:41.145 --> 00:00:43.061
Can you tell me more about them?"

00:00:43.593 --> 00:00:45.237
It always works,

00:00:45.261 --> 00:00:47.447
and we have a good laugh about it too.

00:00:48.164 --> 00:00:52.656
In reality, our capital city,
Ulaanbaatar, is very urban.

00:00:53.267 --> 00:00:54.681
We have commercial buildings,

00:00:54.705 --> 00:00:55.877
brand-name hotels

00:00:55.901 --> 00:00:57.773
and beautiful art spaces too.

00:00:58.078 --> 00:01:00.536
But all too often

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:04.352
foreigners fixate on what Mongolia lacks.

00:01:05.006 --> 00:01:07.853
They look at our massive,
untouched landscape,

00:01:07.877 --> 00:01:10.195
traditional nomadic lifestyles,

00:01:10.219 --> 00:01:12.987
and see it as a sign of poverty.

00:01:13.524 --> 00:01:15.150
And I disagree.

00:01:15.174 --> 00:01:17.624
In fact, I think there's
a lot we can learn

00:01:17.648 --> 00:01:19.783
from ancient Mongolian nomads

00:01:19.807 --> 00:01:21.030
that will help us survive

00:01:21.054 --> 00:01:23.534
in the years and decades to come.

00:01:24.837 --> 00:01:29.901
This is a picture of me playing Mongolia's
most celebrated traditional instrument,

00:01:29.925 --> 00:01:31.266
morin khuur,

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:33.705
or horsehead fiddle.

00:01:34.062 --> 00:01:36.963
I started playing the instrument
when I was only nine,

00:01:36.987 --> 00:01:39.916
and by 11 I was traveling the world

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:42.934
representing Mongolia
at international festivals,

00:01:42.958 --> 00:01:49.438
living and studying in places like Japan,
China, Finland, Germany and Sweden.

00:01:49.818 --> 00:01:51.649
But then suddenly,

00:01:51.673 --> 00:01:53.298
when I was 21,

00:01:53.322 --> 00:01:55.767
I lost my loving mother,

00:01:55.791 --> 00:01:57.530
and just two years later

00:01:57.554 --> 00:01:59.095
I lost my father.

00:01:59.952 --> 00:02:01.731
As an only child,

00:02:01.755 --> 00:02:04.032
I was devastated and lonely.

00:02:04.881 --> 00:02:08.781
At the time, the only thing
I had left was my country,

00:02:08.805 --> 00:02:11.220
so I decided to move home.

00:02:12.028 --> 00:02:13.895
When I was lost with sorrow,

00:02:13.919 --> 00:02:17.757
my country gave me a feeling
of safety and belonging.

00:02:18.268 --> 00:02:22.094
I imagined eternal the blue sky
of Mongolia as my father

00:02:22.118 --> 00:02:25.040
and the untouched, gorgeous
landscape as my mother.

00:02:26.306 --> 00:02:29.709
Having lived in developed countries
for over a decade,

00:02:29.733 --> 00:02:33.368
I became very distant
from the nomadic lifestyles,

00:02:33.392 --> 00:02:37.415
so I wanted to reconnect
and experience it for myself.

00:02:37.902 --> 00:02:41.975
I often journeyed away from the city
toward my grandparents' provinces

00:02:41.999 --> 00:02:44.043
in rural Mongolia

00:02:44.067 --> 00:02:46.658
to see where my parents and I came from,

00:02:46.682 --> 00:02:49.006
and better understand my own identity.

00:02:49.498 --> 00:02:54.331
Growing up, I'd always heard stories
about how Mongolian nomads

00:02:54.355 --> 00:02:58.020
were the most hospitable people on earth,

00:02:58.044 --> 00:03:00.387
and I wanted to see with my own eyes

00:03:00.411 --> 00:03:04.016
whether they really feed
and give shelter to a stranger.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:06.904
So I set off to the countryside,

00:03:06.928 --> 00:03:09.897
driving along dirt roads for hours.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:12.991
What's incredible about Mongolian nomads

00:03:13.015 --> 00:03:18.343
is that the neighbors are often
40 kilometers apart,

00:03:18.367 --> 00:03:22.467
and there's no private land ownership
of pasture land in Mongolia.

00:03:23.006 --> 00:03:24.332
In a way,

00:03:24.356 --> 00:03:27.879
Mongolian nomads
have the complete freedom,

00:03:27.903 --> 00:03:30.797
moving about the gorgeous
landscape as they wish.

00:03:30.821 --> 00:03:33.427
Eventually, I spotted to humble yurts

00:03:33.451 --> 00:03:35.040
and I pulled over.

00:03:35.064 --> 00:03:37.046
Yurts, or ger,

00:03:37.070 --> 00:03:39.189
are a traditional Mongolian dwelling.

00:03:39.529 --> 00:03:43.224
They're made from one hundred
percent natural material,

00:03:43.248 --> 00:03:44.973
a wooden frame and floor,

00:03:44.997 --> 00:03:46.149
leather rope

00:03:46.173 --> 00:03:49.355
and thick blankets
made from felted sheep's wool.

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:52.491
And it takes about only
three to four hours

00:03:52.515 --> 00:03:55.292
to assemble or disassemble,

00:03:55.316 --> 00:03:57.000
and keeps them warm

00:03:57.024 --> 00:04:00.468
through the minus 50 degree
Celsius winters.

00:04:01.738 --> 00:04:02.991
Outside the yurt,

00:04:03.015 --> 00:04:06.102
the kids were playing
with sheep and goats,

00:04:06.126 --> 00:04:07.578
and as I greeted them,

00:04:07.602 --> 00:04:09.688
their parents welcomed me inside.

00:04:10.137 --> 00:04:12.814
The wife poured me nice warm milk tea,

00:04:12.838 --> 00:04:14.325
and the husband offered me food

00:04:14.349 --> 00:04:16.760
that they had already
prepared on the table.

00:04:17.213 --> 00:04:19.048
After some casual chitchat,

00:04:19.072 --> 00:04:21.770
the husband politely asked my purpose,

00:04:21.794 --> 00:04:23.108
so I replied bluntly

00:04:23.132 --> 00:04:24.816
that I was just traveling

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:27.205
and exploring my grandparents' roots

00:04:27.229 --> 00:04:29.260
and that I needed a place to stay

00:04:29.284 --> 00:04:31.332
as the sun was setting.

00:04:31.356 --> 00:04:32.604
And guess what?

00:04:33.095 --> 00:04:36.486
He said I could stay
as long as I needed to,

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:38.126
on one condition.

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:41.561
He asked if I would play the morin khuur,

00:04:41.585 --> 00:04:44.372
our traditional Mongolian
horsehead fiddle.

00:04:45.431 --> 00:04:48.608
In my head, I couldn't believe
it was coming true.

00:04:48.949 --> 00:04:51.599
And the horsehead fiddle
was like a ticket.

00:04:52.104 --> 00:04:55.229
When Mongolians find out
that you can play morin khuur,

00:04:55.253 --> 00:04:57.323
you're instantly respected.

00:04:58.093 --> 00:05:00.205
They say its two strings

00:05:00.229 --> 00:05:02.897
express all the events of the world.

00:05:03.676 --> 00:05:06.951
I ended up staying
with them for nine days,

00:05:06.975 --> 00:05:09.083
and they didn't even ask me to leave.

00:05:09.469 --> 00:05:11.804
I think if I tried
to stay there for two months,

00:05:11.828 --> 00:05:13.309
they would have let me.

00:05:13.694 --> 00:05:15.243
And here's the thing:

00:05:15.267 --> 00:05:16.911
before I met them,

00:05:16.935 --> 00:05:21.833
I assumed that Mongolian nomads
were hospitable out of kindness

00:05:21.857 --> 00:05:23.565
like anybody else.

00:05:23.589 --> 00:05:26.503
But then I realized it was more than that.

00:05:27.132 --> 00:05:30.180
It was about surviving as a community.

00:05:30.204 --> 00:05:34.459
Because nomads live
in extremely remote areas,

00:05:34.483 --> 00:05:37.056
they are completely
at the mercy of nature.

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:40.048
Heavy snowfall,

00:05:40.072 --> 00:05:41.429
a sudden flood

00:05:41.453 --> 00:05:42.998
or a raging storm

00:05:43.022 --> 00:05:45.210
can devastate a nomadic family.

00:05:46.049 --> 00:05:49.874
Today, it's a stranger who needs help,

00:05:49.898 --> 00:05:52.516
but tomorrow, it could be you.

00:05:52.869 --> 00:05:55.028
That's why they look out for each other

00:05:55.052 --> 00:05:57.992
and welcome anyone in need of help.

00:05:58.485 --> 00:06:00.915
This really touched my heart,

00:06:00.939 --> 00:06:04.940
because I feel like we humans
are becoming more and more selfish.

00:06:06.070 --> 00:06:09.611
Staying with a truly nomadic
family awakened me.

00:06:09.635 --> 00:06:12.630
It was nothing like I've ever seen
in developed countries.

00:06:13.136 --> 00:06:14.541
The wife of the family

00:06:14.565 --> 00:06:18.824
showed me how they produce
organic dairy products from scratch,

00:06:18.848 --> 00:06:21.199
like white cheese, yogurt, tsegee,

00:06:21.223 --> 00:06:24.636
and even a traditional vodka
made from cow milk.

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:30.436
And every tool they use
is made from natural material by hand.

00:06:31.278 --> 00:06:32.680
And inside the yurt,

00:06:32.704 --> 00:06:36.045
we burned dried cow dung to stay warm

00:06:36.069 --> 00:06:37.586
instead of using fuel.

00:06:38.293 --> 00:06:40.955
Everything stood in sharp contrast

00:06:40.979 --> 00:06:44.148
to my city life
filled with plastic and steel.

00:06:44.948 --> 00:06:49.055
And this was a five-senses
experience to me,

00:06:49.079 --> 00:06:52.120
a completely different
form of sophistication.

00:06:52.922 --> 00:06:57.668
The more I traveled across remote
and rural destinations in Mongolia,

00:06:57.692 --> 00:06:59.045
the more I understood

00:06:59.069 --> 00:07:03.794
how ancient nomadic lifestyle
was powered by Mother Nature.

00:07:04.464 --> 00:07:08.126
Nomadic life is truly zero waste.

00:07:08.991 --> 00:07:10.992
Over the course of six years,

00:07:11.016 --> 00:07:14.081
I visited more than 20 families,

00:07:14.105 --> 00:07:17.068
and my experience was always the same.

00:07:17.092 --> 00:07:19.952
They invited me in, offered me food

00:07:19.976 --> 00:07:22.529
and gave me a place to stay
if I needed it.

00:07:23.181 --> 00:07:26.577
I was surprised by how little they owned.

00:07:26.601 --> 00:07:30.506
At first, I thought it was because
they moved about four times a year.

00:07:30.530 --> 00:07:33.121
OK, that's a very simple
logic to understand.

00:07:33.145 --> 00:07:35.035
You only carry what you need.

00:07:35.356 --> 00:07:36.769
But then I learned

00:07:36.793 --> 00:07:39.299
there's a deeper philosophy behind it.

00:07:39.857 --> 00:07:42.649
Historically, nomads believed

00:07:42.673 --> 00:07:45.138
that we are only
passing through this life,

00:07:45.162 --> 00:07:47.345
that people come and leave naked,

00:07:47.369 --> 00:07:52.821
so they believe that there's no point
in building anything that destroys nature

00:07:52.845 --> 00:07:56.193
or in being greedy
for materialistic things

00:07:56.217 --> 00:08:00.277
when your life expectancy
is only less than 100 years.

00:08:01.031 --> 00:08:03.639
Instead, they invest in tradition,

00:08:03.663 --> 00:08:05.498
heritage, history,

00:08:05.522 --> 00:08:08.299
and pass it from generation to generation.

00:08:08.873 --> 00:08:13.903
This ancient nomadic philosophy
made me realize that I should think bigger

00:08:13.927 --> 00:08:17.184
and further than my own
convenience and comfort.

00:08:18.252 --> 00:08:25.136
In the Mongolian countryside,
I felt a true form of freedom,

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:27.545
and every time I came back to the city,

00:08:27.569 --> 00:08:30.092
I looked for ways to live more minimally.

00:08:30.535 --> 00:08:33.612
I digitalized all of my company's
paper procedures.

00:08:33.948 --> 00:08:37.220
What once took 20 packs of A4 paper

00:08:37.244 --> 00:08:38.747
now takes just one.

00:08:39.253 --> 00:08:42.681
I downsized my apartment,
reduced my carbon footprint

00:08:42.705 --> 00:08:45.284
and picked up a habit
to rethink my actions,

00:08:45.308 --> 00:08:48.038
like purchasing, choosing transportation,

00:08:48.062 --> 00:08:51.648
and many other lifestyle choices
at home and work.

00:08:52.348 --> 00:08:54.067
And most importantly,

00:08:54.091 --> 00:08:58.062
I stopped working on fast-moving
consumer-goods marketing projects

00:08:58.086 --> 00:09:02.932
and now work with organizations
that promote sustainability.

00:09:02.956 --> 00:09:07.386
But by far the biggest change

00:09:07.410 --> 00:09:09.715
is that I've started to see development

00:09:09.739 --> 00:09:11.573
with fresh eyes.

00:09:12.404 --> 00:09:14.086
In cities,

00:09:14.110 --> 00:09:16.927
living in a traditional yurt as a nomad

00:09:16.951 --> 00:09:18.760
and having less

00:09:18.784 --> 00:09:23.262
is often interpreted as a sign of poverty,

00:09:23.286 --> 00:09:25.006
not just abroad

00:09:25.030 --> 00:09:27.561
but at home in Mongolia too.

00:09:27.904 --> 00:09:31.972
We think that the end goal
for every developing country

00:09:31.996 --> 00:09:34.673
is to become the next Tokyo
or New York City,

00:09:34.697 --> 00:09:38.691
with their skyscrapers,
big shopping malls and toll roads.

00:09:39.355 --> 00:09:43.318
Communities around the world are
abandoning their traditional lifestyles

00:09:43.342 --> 00:09:45.339
in pursuit of material wealth.

00:09:45.870 --> 00:09:47.537
But let's not forget,

00:09:47.561 --> 00:09:49.125
the developed countries

00:09:49.149 --> 00:09:53.320
are the ones most responsible
for climate change.

00:09:53.750 --> 00:09:56.177
So we have to ask ourselves,

00:09:56.201 --> 00:09:59.766
why do we keep on following
the same blueprint

00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:02.549
when we know it causes harm to the world?

00:10:03.086 --> 00:10:06.398
We've all experienced
the consequences of our choices

00:10:06.422 --> 00:10:08.387
over the past eight months.

00:10:08.934 --> 00:10:11.531
So doing right by Mother Nature

00:10:11.555 --> 00:10:15.366
and focusing on earth-friendly,
zero-waste habits

00:10:15.390 --> 00:10:17.519
is not an option anymore.

00:10:18.056 --> 00:10:22.296
And who knows the key ingredients
better than our ancestors,

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:25.239
the ones who survived without the media

00:10:25.263 --> 00:10:26.745
or technology

00:10:26.769 --> 00:10:28.684
but with wisdom alone?

00:10:29.690 --> 00:10:32.009
As a citizen of Mongolia,

00:10:32.033 --> 00:10:33.604
I grew up hearing

00:10:33.628 --> 00:10:37.051
that developing countries are inferior,

00:10:37.075 --> 00:10:39.111
and I really took it to heart.

00:10:39.811 --> 00:10:44.489
But today, I want to say loud and clear

00:10:44.513 --> 00:10:48.553
that I don't see disadvantages
from developing countries anymore.

00:10:48.893 --> 00:10:50.429
On the contrary,

00:10:50.453 --> 00:10:53.846
I see countries that have
the biggest opportunity

00:10:53.870 --> 00:10:56.002
to do things in the right way,

00:10:56.026 --> 00:11:00.064
countries that can define
their own kind of development

00:11:00.088 --> 00:11:04.469
and have the most advantage
to build a better and safer environment

00:11:04.493 --> 00:11:05.905
for everyone.

00:11:05.929 --> 00:11:09.420
What worked for our ancestors
for thousands of years

00:11:09.444 --> 00:11:10.731
can work for us now,

00:11:10.755 --> 00:11:12.215
and in the future,

00:11:12.239 --> 00:11:14.825
when combined with the latest innovations.

00:11:15.560 --> 00:11:19.318
After all, we're all guests in this world,

00:11:19.342 --> 00:11:23.071
so let's do right
by the earth and each other

00:11:23.095 --> 00:11:26.182
just like the ancient
Mongolian nomads did.

00:11:26.550 --> 00:11:27.706
Thank you.

